@Layer.core
Feature: spec/permutations holds BitPerm's mappings, grammars and cost presets

  Scenario: The generated tree validates
    Given the documents generated by "polypiler-tooling permutations import-bitperm --fixtures tests/fixtures/bitperm --out spec/permutations"
    When I run "polypiler-tooling spec validate --family permutations"
    Then the exit code is 0
    And the last output line is "74 documents, 0 errors"

  Scenario Outline: A cost document carries exactly the preset's available operations
    Given the document "spec/permutations/costs/bitperm-<preset>.yaml"
    Then primitive "<primitive>" has cycles <cycles>

    Examples:
      | preset   | primitive    | cycles |
      | modern   | pext         | 3      |
      | zen1_2   | pdep         | 18     |
      | 6502     | lookup-table | 5      |
      | arm_a64  | bit-reverse  | 1      |
      | gamecube | rotate-mask  | 1      |

  Scenario: An unavailable operation is absent, not expensive
    Given the document "spec/permutations/costs/bitperm-6502.yaml"
    Then it lists no cost for "multiply", "pext", "pdep", "byte-shuffle" or "byte-swap"
    And no cost in any generated document has cycles 999

  Scenario: A mapping document that disagrees with BitPerm's raw evaluation is refused
    Given "spec/permutations/mappings/bitperm-pattern-reverse-8.yaml" with the vector output for input "0x01" changed from "0x80" to "0x40"
    When I run "polypiler-tooling spec validate --family permutations"
    Then stderr contains "the mapping gives 0x80 for input 0x01, the document says 0x40 (rule: vector-consistent)"
    And the exit code is 1

  Scenario: A new BitPerm cost key stops the import
    Given a copy of "presets.json" in which preset "modern" has the additional cost key "clmul"
    When I run the importer on it
    Then stderr contains "permutations import: preset 'modern' has unknown cost key 'clmul'"
    And the exit code is 1
